# tools/s/status_declared_witness.rish -- a lap that leaves no status cannot be told from one that was killed.
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/s/status_declared_witness.rish
#
# `.claude/rules/session-log-provenance.md` asks every log to finish with `scope` and `status` for
# this hand, and `status` answers what nothing else in the record answers: did the lap consider
# itself green when it stopped? Measured `20260907.200326`: of 95 logs on that day's shelf, 21
# carried both fields and 73 carried neither.
#
# THE GAP CAME FROM AN INCIDENT RATHER THAN AN AUDIT. An incense loop was cancelled by hand at the
# moment its lap completed. The work stood staged and whole and the log claimed no send -- so
# nothing was dishonest -- yet nothing in the record said whether the lap had been INTERRUPTED or
# had FINISHED AND DECLINED to send. Two different facts, two different repairs, one appearance.
#
# REPORTED, NEVER GATED, and the incident is the argument rather than a preference: a lap killed
# mid-send cannot write a status by definition, so a gate would red hardest on exactly the laps that
# were interrupted -- punishing the accident it exists to make visible.

say "status-declared: Language EN -- one question the record could not answer about itself."
say "status-declared: Style Gauge, Meter -- a census that reports rather than refuses."
say "status-declared: Lens TAME -- each bucket proven by moving a member into its neighbour."

say "status-declared: the control -- eleven behaviors on planted logs in a throwaway repository..."
let ctl = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/s/status_declared_control.sh"]
# invariant: both streams are written out BARE, ahead of the asserts that judge them. A bare `say`
# opens no `StrBuf`, so the report cannot refuse however large the output grows; an `else` clause
# interpolating the same value composes only on FAILURE, which is the one moment it is owed
# (`tools/s/say_compose_bound_witness.rish`).
say ctl.out
say ctl.err
assert ctl.ok else "status-declared: the control refused -- its two streams stand above"
assert ctl.out contains "pass=11 fail=0" else "status-declared: the control did not prove eleven behaviors -- its two streams stand above"

say "status-declared: the open day's shelf answers every bucket..."
let scan = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/s/status_declared_scan.sh"]
say scan.out
assert scan.ok else "status-declared: the scan refused -- its reading stands above"
# invariant: every bucket is reported, empty or full. A reading that printed only the fault would
# leave a reader unable to tell a healthy day from a day the scan could not see.
assert scan.out contains "both=" else "status-declared: the fully-declared count went unreported"
assert scan.out contains "neither=" else "status-declared: the undeclared count went unreported"
assert scan.out contains "scope_only=" else "status-declared: the scope-only count went unreported"
assert scan.out contains "status_only=" else "status-declared: the status-only count went unreported"

say "status-declared: and the field the census counts is named by the law..."
let seated = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'scope. and .status' .claude/rules/session-log-provenance.md || true"]
assert seated.ok else "status-declared: the provenance law could not be read"
# invariant: a field a census counts and no law names is a convention, and a convention cannot be
# followed by a ship that never met it -- the very gap this census measures.
assert (trim seated.out) != "0" else "status-declared: the status field is counted by a census and named by no law"

say "GREEN: status-declared -- a lap's own verdict has a field, the law names it, and an interrupted lap is told from a finished one."
